00:35:33,992 --> 00:35:35,591
She is eating it today thanks to you.

1037
00:35:35,592 --> 00:35:37,422
(Proud)

1038
00:35:38,862 --> 00:35:42,032
(Thanks to the considerate choice in the menu, )

1039
00:35:43,292 --> 00:35:45,001
(Ju Seung's mom enjoys the tender eels.)

1040
00:35:45,002 --> 00:35:46,301
I can tell that she likes them.

1041
00:35:46,302 --> 00:35:48,702
Next month, the two of us will go and eat them.

1042
00:35:48,832 --> 00:35:51,042
- Eat what? Eels? - We'll go and eat eels.

1043
00:35:51,242 --> 00:35:52,341
- You and Ju Seung's mom? - Ju Seung's mom.

1044
00:35:52,342 --> 00:35:53,711
- Without Ju Seung? - Ju Seung won't go.

1045
00:35:53,712 --> 00:35:55,141
(Ju Seung won't go.)

1046
00:35:55,142 --> 00:35:56,741
- Really? - Will she go and eat eels...

1047
00:35:56,742 --> 00:35:58,041
with you, not Ju Seung?

1048
00:35:58,042 --> 00:35:59,612
They've talked about eating eels...

1049
00:35:59,942 --> 00:36:01,612
- for months. - It has been months.

1050
00:36:02,082 --> 00:36:03,211
But Sung Hwan postponed it.

1051
00:36:03,212 --> 00:36:05,122
Ju Seung's mom and I drink often.

1052
00:36:05,182 --> 00:36:07,851
We drink at Ju Seung's place when he's not around.

1053
00:36:07,852 --> 00:36:09,391
- Then I go, "I'll get going now." - Oh, my.

1054
00:36:09,392 --> 00:36:10,621
We do that.

1055
00:36:10,622 --> 00:36:11,661
- That's so nice. - We eat together often too.

1056
00:36:11,662 --> 00:36:13,321
- It's so fascinating. - I bet they get along well.

1057
00:36:13,322 --> 00:36:14,392
That's the best.

1058
00:36:15,162 --> 00:36:16,361
(She enjoys eels with her family...)

1059
00:36:16,362 --> 00:36:17,662
(before the meeting with her friend.)

1060
00:36:17,962 --> 00:36:19,462
(Glancing over)

1061
00:36:20,032 --> 00:36:21,872
(She eats a piece of eel just like her mother-in-law.)

1062
00:36:22,132 --> 00:36:23,671
The eels are tasty.

1063
00:36:23,672 --> 00:36:25,072
- Hundred points? - Hundred points.

1064
00:36:26,172 --> 00:36:27,912
How was the Korean Folk Village?

1065
00:36:28,172 --> 00:36:29,442
It was so much fun.

1066
00:36:30,242 --> 00:36:31,381
How were the photos?

1067
00:36:31,382 --> 00:36:33,452
You took incredibly pretty photos.

1068
00:36:33,852 --> 00:36:35,082
Do the photos look okay?

1069
00:36:35,152 --> 00:36:36,582
Ju Seung takes nice photos.

1070
00:36:37,182 --> 00:36:38,621
- What's this? - Did I take them well?

1071
00:36:38,622 --> 00:36:39,622
(Getting up)

1072
00:36:40,452 --> 00:36:41,792
That's a nice photo.

1073
00:36:42,622 --> 00:36:43,962
You look so pretty.

1074
00:36:44,392 --> 00:36:45,722
That's an amazing photo.

1075
00:36:46,522 --> 00:36:47,661
(She stares at the photo because she likes it.)

1076
00:36:47,662 --> 00:36:49,292
- It's a pretty photo. - It's a nice shot.

1077
00:36:49,962 --> 00:36:51,601
- You can... - It's the best.

1078
00:36:51,602 --> 00:36:52,801
You can edit it later.

1079
00:36:52,802 --> 00:36:54,231
Is she editing it already?

1080
00:36:54,232 --> 00:36:57,342
She's editing it to make it the best photo.

1081
00:36:58,302 --> 00:37:00,442
Apparently, it takes one minute to edit a single photo.

1082
00:37:00,642 --> 00:37:02,511
- It takes no time. - If she waits, she'll forget.

1083
00:37:02,512 --> 00:37:03,512
That's right.

1084
00:37:03,742 --> 00:37:06,142
Why did you stretch the legs so much and make yourself 180cm tall?

1085
00:37:07,182 --> 00:37:09,012
- Okay. - It looks good.

1086
00:37:09,382 --> 00:37:10,682
It's a nice photo.

1087
00:37:12,082 --> 00:37:14,392
- It's a nice photo. - It really is.

1088
00:37:15,622 --> 00:37:17,121
I captured...

1089
00:37:17,122 --> 00:37:18,861
the sky and trees perfectly.

1090
00:37:18,862 --> 00:37:20,332
It's the best photo.

1091
00:37:20,662 --> 00:37:23,491
- If you were going to edit it, - Send it to me.

1092
00:37:23,492 --> 00:37:25,132
One photo would've been enough.

1093
00:37:25,932 --> 00:37:27,631
(Smiling)

1094
00:37:27,632 --> 00:37:29,932
You take 1,000 photos and get 1 good one.

1095
00:37:30,572 --> 00:37:31,942
Send me the photos.

1096
00:37:32,502 --> 00:37:34,372
- I sent them to you. - Okay.

1097
00:37:35,112 --> 00:37:38,042
Now that my sister-in-law joined the family,

1098
00:37:38,142 --> 00:37:40,142
we gained a family member.

1099
00:37:40,482 --> 00:37:41,551
I feel like...

1100
00:37:41,552 --> 00:37:43,652
- the family became more amicable. - That's right.

1101
00:37:43,982 --> 00:37:45,652
My mom and I...

1102
00:37:45,852 --> 00:37:49,152
don't go out by ourselves often.

1103
00:37:49,652 --> 00:37:53,632
Thanks to this excuse, I got to travel with my mom.

1104
00:37:54,132 --> 00:37:56,932
My sister-in-law and I spent time together...

1105
00:37:57,832 --> 00:38:00,832
and got closer.

1106
00:38:01,132 --> 00:38:02,502
It was rewarding.

1107
00:38:05,102 --> 00:38:07,672
- Ramyeon is a must when you camp. - You have to eat ramyeon.

1108
00:38:09,772 --> 00:38:11,381
It looks so tasty.

1109
00:38:11,382 --> 00:38:12,642
It's the tastiest.

1110
00:38:13,382 --> 00:38:14,412
My goodness.

1111
00:38:14,552 --> 00:38:16,411
At a place like that, you can eat ramyeon even when you're full.

1112
00:38:16,412 --> 00:38:17,522
- Yes, you can. - Of course.

1113
00:38:18,952 --> 00:38:21,092
(Ju Seung's sister-in-law slurps the noodles as well.)

1114
00:38:21,452 --> 00:38:22,592
It's tasty.

1115
00:38:22,922 --> 00:38:24,092
Is it tasty?

1116
00:38:24,792 --> 00:38:27,331
When you visit in winter, let's go skiing together...

1117
00:38:27,332 --> 00:38:28,462
if you have time.

1118
00:38:28,632 --> 00:38:29,731
- That sounds good. - Yes.

1119
00:38:29,732 --> 00:38:31,432
My brother should come as well.

1120
00:38:32,302 --> 00:38:33,402
(I Live Alone)

1121
00:38:34,372 --> 00:38:36,371
(Late Summer Greetings)

1122
00:38:36,372 --> 00:38:37,842
Autumn is coming at last.

1123
00:38:38,642 --> 00:38:40,442
(KEY is going somewhere in a car.)

1124
00:38:41,242 --> 00:38:42,941
(Is he headed to his parents' place?)

1125
00:38:42,942 --> 00:38:45,511
- Did you drive to Daegu? - Yes, I drove there.

1126
00:38:45,512 --> 00:38:46,881
- It has been a while, right? - Yes.

1127
00:38:46,882 --> 00:38:48,752
(He drives past the farming area under the cloudy sky.)

1128
00:38:50,212 --> 00:38:51,681
I drove past Daegu and went to Yeongcheon.

1129
00:38:51,682 --> 00:38:52,852
(He drives past Daegu and goes to Yeongcheon.)

1130
00:38:55,952 --> 00:38:58,722
It's rare for you not to listen to music...

1131
00:38:59,192 --> 00:39:00,462
- during the drive. - You're right.

1132
00:39:01,192 --> 00:39:02,332
Where is he going?

1133
00:39:02,862 --> 00:39:04,632
I'm here.

1134
00:39:08,302 --> 00:39:10,942
(Is it raining?)

1135
00:39:12,302 --> 00:39:13,472
It isn't raining.

1136
00:39:15,542 --> 00:39:17,811
(He carries something with both hands.)

1137
00:39:17,812 --> 00:39:18,942
What?

1138
00:39:19,612 --> 00:39:22,012
(He walks over to someplace.)

1139
00:39:22,482 --> 00:39:25,781
(A resting place for the patriots)

1140
00:39:25,782 --> 00:39:29,822
It's a national cemetery in Yeongcheon.

1141
00:39:35,392 --> 00:39:38,802
This place is where the war veterans,

1142
00:39:39,002 --> 00:39:41,031
soldiers,

1143
00:39:41,032 --> 00:39:44,702
and people of national merit rest.

1144
00:39:46,072 --> 00:39:48,772
My grandparents are buried here.

1145
00:39:49,542 --> 00:39:51,142
My paternal grandparents.

1146
00:39:52,342 --> 00:39:56,052
My grandpa fought in the Korean War.

1147
00:39:56,552 --> 00:39:57,851
- That's impressive. - He was discharged...

1148
00:39:57,852 --> 00:39:59,122
due to an injury.

1149
00:39:59,382 --> 00:40:02,622
He took a bullet in his ankle.

1150
00:40:03,522 --> 00:40:07,362
For his nation, he fought with all his might.

1151
00:40:07,832 --> 00:40:10,461
I realized much later...

1152
00:40:10,462 --> 00:40:14,131
that only a select few received...

1153
00:40:14,132 --> 00:40:18,202
recognition from the nation.

1154
00:40:19,472 --> 00:40:23,242
September is a meaningful month for me,

1155
00:40:23,542 --> 00:40:25,181
so I visited them to say hello...

1156
00:40:25,182 --> 00:40:27,212
for the first time in a long while.

1157
00:40:28,152 --> 00:40:31,421
My father is also a man of merit. He fought in the Vietnam War.

1158
00:40:31,422 --> 00:40:32,681
- Did he fight in the Vietnam War? - Yes.

1159
00:40:32,682 --> 00:40:34,951
My hometown is the Sipjaseong Town.

1160
00:40:34,952 --> 00:40:36,052
Sipjaseong Town.

1161
00:40:36,092 --> 00:40:38,962
(The place where he met his neighbor who is a war veteran, Mr. Ddol)

1162
00:40:39,762 --> 00:40:43,532
(Sipjaseong Town is a town for the people of national merit.)

1163
00:40:44,732 --> 00:40:48,932
(Many neighbors live with injuries from the war.)

1164
00:40:49,532 --> 00:40:51,141
My paternal grandpa fought in the Korean War.

1165
00:40:51,142 --> 00:40:53,271
His younger brother was an officer during the Korean War,

1166
00:40:53,272 --> 00:40:56,042
so he's buried in a different national cemetery.

1167
00:40:56,612 --> 00:40:59,182
My maternal grandpa fought in the Vietnam War.

1168
00:40:59,382 --> 00:41:00,852
He's buried in a different cemetery in Daejeon.

1169
00:41:00,982 --> 00:41:02,412
You come from a family of patriots.

1170
00:41:02,582 --> 00:41:05,052
- That's right. You must have pride. - I have pride.

1171
00:41:05,152 --> 00:41:06,781
I did my military service at the Seoul National Cemetery.

1172
00:41:06,782 --> 00:41:08,291
That's right.

1173
00:41:08,292 --> 00:41:09,551
- You're a patriotic idol. - Yes.

1174
00:41:09,552 --> 00:41:11,392
- "A patriotic idol." - "A patriotic idol."

1175
00:41:11,692 --> 00:41:13,491
You should perform at national events.

1176
00:41:13,492 --> 00:41:16,332
- "A patriotic idol." - Hey, we'll give you a gold badge.

1177
00:41:17,432 --> 00:41:19,001
Give me a patriot's badge.

1178
00:41:19,002 --> 00:41:21,171
- Seriously. Thank you, KEY. - Thank you.

1179
00:41:21,172 --> 00:41:22,202
(Kian84 means his words.)

1180
00:41:25,342 --> 00:41:27,342
(He drops by at a place before visiting his grandparents.)

1181
00:41:27,842 --> 00:41:29,142
Hello.

1182
00:41:30,812 --> 00:41:33,611
(They sell silk flowers for the graves.)

1183
00:41:33,612 --> 00:41:35,512
(Staring)

1184
00:41:36,252 --> 00:41:38,251
- Are those real flowers? - Yes.

1185
00:41:38,252 --> 00:41:40,222
People use silk flowers for the graves in the mountain...

1186
00:41:40,422 --> 00:41:41,821
and use real flowers for the columbarium.

1187
00:41:41,822 --> 00:41:42,991
- It must be because of the weather. - Yes.

1188
00:41:42,992 --> 00:41:45,662
- It rains outside. - That's right.

1189
00:41:46,062 --> 00:41:48,692
- Silk flowers look good for years. - That's right.

1190
00:41:50,862 --> 00:41:53,602
(Since these flowers are for his grandparents)

1191
00:41:54,332 --> 00:41:56,332
- So... - That's right. Make a big bouquet.

1192
00:41:57,702 --> 00:41:59,701
(He combines the colors carefully.)

1193
00:41:59,702 --> 00:42:00,772
Hold on.

1194
00:42:02,072 --> 00:42:04,081
- They're pretty. - Will this be okay?

1195
00:42:04,082 --> 00:42:06,042
Yes. That looks good.

1196
00:42:07,782 --> 00:42:09,581
(He holds the flowers that will never wither.)

1197
00:42:09,582 --> 00:42:11,051
"The flowers that will never wither."

1198
00:42:11,052 --> 00:42:13,682
- Bye. - Take care.

1199
00:42:14,892 --> 00:42:17,922
(He goes to see his grandparents now.)

1200
00:42:19,492 --> 00:42:22,132
(He holds the flowers in his arms.)

1201
00:42:22,662 --> 00:42:24,102
Their graves were pretty far up.

1202
00:42:26,402 --> 00:42:28,972
(He goes through the past memories of visiting them...)

1203
00:42:29,702 --> 00:42:31,001
(and walks over to them.)

1204
00:42:31,002 --> 00:42:32,202
You did a good thing.

1205
00:42:35,542 --> 00:42:37,282
It has been a while.

1206
00:42:40,812 --> 00:42:42,152
It's huge.

1207
00:42:42,912 --> 00:42:46,952
- The place makes you reverent. - That's right.

1208
00:42:47,192 --> 00:42:48,251
We often forget...

1209
00:42:48,252 --> 00:42:50,452
- that we owe our lives to them. - That's right.

1210
00:42:50,922 --> 00:42:53,462
I have so much gratitude and respect for them.

1211
00:42:55,532 --> 00:42:58,832
(He's nearly at his grandparents' graves.)

1212
00:43:01,132 --> 00:43:02,232
There they are.

1213
00:43:04,342 --> 00:43:05,701
Have you been well?

1214
00:43:05,702 --> 00:43:06,842
My goodness.

1215
00:43:07,372 --> 00:43:09,511
(Kim Jae Kwan, Sergeant First Class of the Army)

1216
00:43:09,512 --> 00:43:11,342
Are your grandparents buried at the same place?

1217
00:43:11,742 --> 00:43:14,811
My grandpa's grave was there first. When my grandma passed away,

1218
00:43:14,812 --> 00:43:17,352
- they could be put side by side. - They could do that for them.

1219
00:43:18,052 --> 00:43:20,252
The period my grandpa fought in the war...

1220
00:43:20,752 --> 00:43:21,952
It's written on the tombstone.

1221
00:43:22,122 --> 00:43:23,322
- All the information is there. - Yes.

1222
00:43:23,692 --> 00:43:26,522
He went through a lot.

1223
00:43:27,292 --> 00:43:29,932
Fighting in a war for over a year...

1224
00:43:30,292 --> 00:43:31,431
(My goodness)

1225
00:43:31,432 --> 00:43:34,531
Training for 5 day and 4 nights makes me feel like I'm dying.

1226
00:43:34,532 --> 00:43:35,672
I know.

1227
00:43:35,902 --> 00:43:39,842
You know, there's something I still remember.

1228
00:43:40,142 --> 00:43:42,472
When "Taegukgi: Brotherhood of War" came out,

1229
00:43:42,942 --> 00:43:44,581
my family went to the theater together.

1230
00:43:44,582 --> 00:43:45,781
Grandparents cry while watching that movie.

1231
00:43:45,782 --> 00:43:48,781
My grandpa cried so much that he couldn't watch the movie.

1232
00:43:48,782 --> 00:43:49,851
- Really? - Yes.

1233
00:43:49,852 --> 00:43:53,181
He said the movie didn't capture 1-10th of the brutality...

1234
00:43:53,182 --> 00:43:54,751
- he had experienced. - Of course not.

1235
00:43:54,752 --> 00:43:55,821
You can't show that properly in a movie.

1236
00:43:55,822 --> 00:43:58,692
I was in elementary school then, but the memory is still fresh.

1237
00:44:00,292 --> 00:44:04,062
"That's my younger brother."

1238
00:44:04,262 --> 00:44:06,602
"That's my younger brother."

1239
00:44:06,832 --> 00:44:08,002
That was sudden.

1240
00:44:08,172 --> 00:44:10,601
- It's a line from the movie. - Go and take a nap.

1241
00:44:10,602 --> 00:44:13,141
No, it's a line from "Taegukgi: Brotherhood of War."

1242
00:44:13,142 --> 00:44:15,641
"That's my younger brother." Then he cries.

1243
00:44:15,642 --> 00:44:18,182
It's a scene from the intro of "Taegukgi: Brotherhood of War."

1244
00:44:19,512 --> 00:44:21,812
(The grandparents and their grandson meet for the first time in a while.)

1245
00:44:22,482 --> 00:44:24,522
The rain stopped so that I could say hello.

1246
00:44:25,552 --> 00:44:27,852
- He's using a dialect naturally. - Yes.

1247
00:44:29,922 --> 00:44:33,262
(He gives them the pretty flowers he has prepared.)

1248
00:44:34,662 --> 00:44:36,562
(He hurriedly spreads out a mat on the ground.)

1249
00:44:37,732 --> 00:44:39,002
Have you been well?

1250
00:44:39,402 --> 00:44:40,701
Your grandparents must've been happy.

1251
00:44:40,702 --> 00:44:42,002
Yes, they must've been happy.

1252
00:44:44,702 --> 00:44:47,371
(Next to his grandparents are the grave of a Vietnam War veteran.)

1253
00:44:47,372 --> 00:44:50,342
- We wouldn't be here without them. - That's right.

1254
00:44:51,842 --> 00:44:53,842
(He wipes off the rainwater.)

1255
00:44:54,052 --> 00:44:56,252
It rained every day back then.

1256
00:44:56,882 --> 00:44:58,252
- It stopped at the right moment. - Yes.

1257
00:44:59,052 --> 00:45:00,792
Your grandparents must've helped...

1258
00:45:01,222 --> 00:45:03,052
for your comfort.

1259
00:45:05,222 --> 00:45:07,562
(Their grandson prepared food to show his love.)

1260
00:45:08,262 --> 00:45:09,462
- Dried pollack. - Yes.

1261
00:45:10,332 --> 00:45:13,202
Although it's a simplified version, you have the necessary items.

1262
00:45:16,502 --> 00:45:19,742
(He pours them alcohol for the first time in a while.)

1263
00:45:20,042 --> 00:45:21,412
You guys can't drink much.

1264
00:45:21,912 --> 00:45:23,372
You should share it.

1265
00:45:27,112 --> 00:45:28,281
Are those rings for them?

1266
00:45:28,282 --> 00:45:29,282
(Joking)

1267
00:45:29,283 --> 00:45:32,451
- Hold on. It's my first time... - Will they like the rings?

1268
00:45:32,452 --> 00:45:34,551
Seeing someone put rings on the table for the dead.

1269
00:45:34,552 --> 00:45:36,522
- Are they for your grandparents? - No.

1270
00:45:36,822 --> 00:45:37,892
You put them on the table.

1271
00:45:37,992 --> 00:45:41,161
- They did like jewelry. - If they liked it...

1272
00:45:41,162 --> 00:45:43,261
- Some people might misunderstand. - Right.

1273
00:45:43,262 --> 00:45:44,932
- I took them off to say hello. - I see.

1274
00:45:47,702 --> 00:45:48,971
(He shows his manners in front of his grandparents.)

1275
00:45:48,972 --> 00:45:50,101
My goodness.

1276
00:45:50,102 --> 00:45:51,242
(He shows his manners in front of his grandparents.)

1277
00:45:53,272 --> 00:45:56,412
(He bows to them for the first time in a while.)

1278
00:46:04,782 --> 00:46:07,621
I bet they cherished him a lot.

1279
00:46:07,622 --> 00:46:08,721
(The grandson they cherished, KEY is here.)

1280
00:46:08,722 --> 00:46:09,852
My goodness.

1281
00:46:13,792 --> 00:46:14,891
My goodness.

1282
00:46:14,892 --> 00:46:17,091
(KEY sits comfortably next to his grandparents.)

1283
00:46:17,092 --> 00:46:19,832
There weren't a lot of people since it was too early.

1284
00:46:22,802 --> 00:46:25,241
When you go there,

1285
00:46:25,242 --> 00:46:26,902
your heart aches. You feel sad.

1286
00:46:28,542 --> 00:46:30,542
They loved me so much.

1287
00:46:34,652 --> 00:46:36,782
In my family,

1288
00:46:44,092 --> 00:46:46,862
both my mom and dad worked.

1289
00:46:47,222 --> 00:46:50,032
So, my grandparents took care of sending me off to school,

1290
00:46:50,192 --> 00:46:52,262
picking me up from school,

1291
00:46:52,432 --> 00:46:54,902
and making my meals.

1292
00:46:55,732 --> 00:46:58,541
Thus, I actually spent more time with my grandparents...

1293
00:46:58,542 --> 00:47:01,942
than my parents in elementary school.

1294
00:47:02,772 --> 00:47:04,982
I loved my grandma's food a lot.

1295
00:47:05,212 --> 00:47:07,712
Even after I grew up,

1296
00:47:08,312 --> 00:47:10,352
I always hung out with my grandma...

1297
00:47:10,452 --> 00:47:12,882
in both Daegu and Seoul.

1298
00:47:13,022 --> 00:47:16,392
I have many memories of such dates.

1299
00:47:17,392 --> 00:47:19,961
(His grandma even watched his debut performance.)

1300
00:47:19,962 --> 00:47:22,232
- How nice. - It was the day I debuted.

1301
00:47:23,762 --> 00:47:27,261
(KEY still remembers...)

1302
00:47:27,262 --> 00:47:29,471
(his Grandma's braised crab in soy sauce.)

1303
00:47:29,472 --> 00:47:31,572
- That's right. You made it. - You told us about it.

1304
00:47:32,402 --> 00:47:35,942
(Now, KEY is big enough to cook it for his parents.)

1305
00:47:36,742 --> 00:47:38,581
(Even when he traveled alone)

1306
00:47:38,582 --> 00:47:39,682
(On Buddha's Birthday?)

1307
00:47:40,212 --> 00:47:41,951
(The word he hasn't heard in a while...)

1308
00:47:41,952 --> 00:47:44,452
(made him immediately think of his grandma.)

1309
00:47:45,682 --> 00:47:48,851
My birthday is in September.

1310
00:47:48,852 --> 00:47:52,391
But my grandma's death anniversary is...

1311
00:47:52,392 --> 00:47:54,862
the same as my birthday.

1312
00:47:55,592 --> 00:47:57,432
She passed away after I reached adulthood.

1313
00:47:57,902 --> 00:48:00,502
For 2 to 3 years after her death,

1314
00:48:00,662 --> 00:48:01,902
I didn't even want to see a cake.

1315
00:48:02,632 --> 00:48:04,741
It was a hectic situation.

1316
00:48:04,742 --> 00:48:06,171
She wasn't sick at all.

1317
00:48:06,172 --> 00:48:08,641
She passed away suddenly in her sleep.

1318
00:48:08,642 --> 00:48:09,911
(His grandma passed away suddenly.)

1319
00:48:09,912 --> 00:48:14,041
For 2 to 3 years, I was so depressed.

1320
00:48:14,042 --> 00:48:15,952
But then I thought,

1321
00:48:16,252 --> 00:48:19,281
"Would she want me to be like this?"

1322
00:48:19,282 --> 00:48:20,622
- Right. - I had that thought.

1323
00:48:20,752 --> 00:48:23,392
Since then, I just go through the past memories...

1324
00:48:24,152 --> 00:48:25,991
- or the happy moments. - That's right.

1325
00:48:25,992 --> 00:48:27,221
Her death anniversary coincided...

1326
00:48:27,222 --> 00:48:28,491
- so that you'd think about her. - That's right.

1327
00:48:28,492 --> 00:48:30,261
The meaning has changed.

1328
00:48:30,262 --> 00:48:32,102
You're such a good grandson.

1329
00:48:32,332 --> 00:48:34,701
In any case, it's still a strange day for me.

1330
00:48:34,702 --> 00:48:35,772
You're right.

1331
00:48:42,612 --> 00:48:44,941
Gosh, it's raining right after greeting them.

1332
00:48:44,942 --> 00:48:47,042
- Is it raining now? - It started to rain.

1333
00:48:49,212 --> 00:48:53,122
(The rain falls as it was waiting right after he greets them.)

1334
00:48:56,052 --> 00:48:58,762
(He sits with them with the umbrella he brought.)

1335
00:49:00,262 --> 00:49:02,092
You look like a kid sitting there like that.

1336
00:49:05,632 --> 00:49:06,902
Well,

1337
00:49:07,262 --> 00:49:08,972
I'm sure it was a massive coincidence,

1338
00:49:09,502 --> 00:49:12,502
but my grandmother hated me being under the rain.

1339
00:49:13,172 --> 00:49:14,541
So whenever it rained,

1340
00:49:14,542 --> 00:49:17,372
she waited for me at school before school ended.

1341
00:49:17,442 --> 00:49:18,481
Every single time.

1342
00:49:18,482 --> 00:49:20,842
- She must've adored you so much. - Right.

1343
00:49:21,612 --> 00:49:25,222
It rained like crazy starting from Seoul.

1344
00:49:25,352 --> 00:49:27,992
It rained endlessly.

1345
00:49:28,192 --> 00:49:29,921
"I won't be able to make deep bows to them with the mat."

1346
00:49:29,922 --> 00:49:32,062
I kept thinking this as I drove, but as I arrived...

1347
00:49:32,162 --> 00:49:33,361
You can't hold onto the umbrella when you make deep bows.

1348
00:49:33,362 --> 00:49:34,431
Right.

1349
00:49:34,432 --> 00:49:36,292
She didn't want her grandson to get wet from the rain.

1350
00:49:36,432 --> 00:49:37,832
- That's fascinating. - Right.

1351
00:49:38,362 --> 00:49:39,931
After seeing it rain again,

1352
00:49:39,932 --> 00:49:42,271
I thought, "You want me to leave now?"

1353
00:49:42,272 --> 00:49:44,802
She wanted you to leave without staying too long.

1354
00:49:44,942 --> 00:49:45,971
She got a good look of you.

1355
00:49:45,972 --> 00:49:48,042
"You want me to come again? Okay."

1356
00:49:49,242 --> 00:49:52,212
Why is it raining so much again all of a sudden?

1357
00:49:53,442 --> 00:49:55,111
Grandmothers always hurry you out.

1358
00:49:55,112 --> 00:49:56,812
- Right. - "Hurry up and go."

1359
00:49:57,052 --> 00:49:58,351
They love it when you stay the night,

1360
00:49:58,352 --> 00:49:59,981
- but they always tell you to go. - Right.

1361
00:49:59,982 --> 00:50:01,952
- They send you away. - Right.

1362
00:50:03,152 --> 00:50:04,692
(He cleans up as his grandmother tells him to.)

1363
00:50:06,192 --> 00:50:08,861
You're not allowed to leave a small amount of food, right?

1364
00:50:08,862 --> 00:50:10,262
You're not allowed to do that.

1365
00:50:11,132 --> 00:50:12,331
Alcoholic drinks can't be thrown out as well.

1366
00:50:12,332 --> 00:50:13,502
You have to take them with you.

1367
00:50:14,002 --> 00:50:15,531
It was called the gosure back then to make small food offerings...

1368
00:50:15,532 --> 00:50:17,131
and food was left for animals to eat.

1369
00:50:17,132 --> 00:50:18,401
The gosure isn't done any more?

1370
00:50:18,402 --> 00:50:20,671
Anything that harms the grave can't be done.

1371
00:50:20,672 --> 00:50:22,012
(The place he stayed for a while gets cleaned up neatly.)

1372
00:50:23,442 --> 00:50:24,612
I'll get going.

1373
00:50:26,612 --> 00:50:28,082
I know how it feels when you say, "I'll get going."

1374
00:50:29,212 --> 00:50:30,381
It breaks your heart.

1375
00:50:30,382 --> 00:50:31,451
(He heads back even though he wants to stay longer.)

1376
00:50:31,452 --> 00:50:33,222
But just paying them a visit...

1377
00:50:33,422 --> 00:50:34,551
would've felt...

1378
00:50:34,552 --> 00:50:36,051
- really refreshing. - Right.

1379
00:50:36,052 --> 00:50:37,622
(Your grandson, Ki Bum, will be back again.)

1380
00:50:38,192 --> 00:50:42,962
(He leaves the national cemetery with his things.)

1381
00:50:44,032 --> 00:50:46,531
- Gosh. - My heart still feels full...

1382
00:50:46,532 --> 00:50:48,101
because I paid a visit to them.

1383
00:50:48,102 --> 00:50:49,432
That must be great.

1384
00:50:49,702 --> 00:50:50,732
You must feel relieved.

1385
00:50:51,772 --> 00:50:54,742
(A short but meaningful meeting with his grandfather and grandmother)

1386
00:50:55,072 --> 00:50:58,042
(Let's see.)

1387
00:50:58,782 --> 00:51:01,052
(He goes past the tollgate of North Yeongcheon.)

1388
00:51:01,212 --> 00:51:02,781
(He drives toward Daegu?)

1389
00:51:02,782 --> 00:51:04,251
You're not heading back to Seoul.

1390
00:51:04,252 --> 00:51:05,282
Right.

1391
00:51:06,122 --> 00:51:09,022
(He gets closer to the center of the city.)

1392
00:51:10,392 --> 00:51:13,162
(It's raining a lot.)

1393
00:51:16,192 --> 00:51:18,062
- It rained a lot. - Right.

1394
00:51:18,732 --> 00:51:20,461
It's raining after Dae Ho washed his car.

1395
00:51:20,462 --> 00:51:22,672
You're right.

1396
00:51:23,672 --> 00:51:27,672
(The rain gets stronger as he arrives.)

1397
00:51:29,742 --> 00:51:31,342
It's raining a lot.

1398
00:51:33,412 --> 00:51:36,452
(Looking around)

1399
00:51:37,952 --> 00:51:41,952
(KEY makes a call to someone while looking around.)

1400
00:51:42,222 --> 00:51:43,352
Mom!

1401
00:51:45,522 --> 00:51:46,992
Welcome.

1402
00:51:48,232 --> 00:51:49,462
Gosh, Mom.

1403
00:51:49,692 --> 00:51:51,831
Gosh, it's been a while since I saw her.

1404
00:51:51,832 --> 00:51:52,832
- Right. - It's been a while.

1405
00:51:52,833 --> 00:51:54,361
- My goodness. - I have to wait so long...

1406
00:51:54,362 --> 00:51:55,972
to see my son.

1407
00:51:57,102 --> 00:51:59,201
(This January, KEY's mother retired...)

1408
00:51:59,202 --> 00:52:02,612
(after working in a hospital for 36 years.)

1409
00:52:02,912 --> 00:52:06,712
(KEY was with her on her last day at work.)

1410
00:52:07,542 --> 00:52:08,951
She went through so much to work for 36 years.

1411
00:52:08,952 --> 00:52:10,581
Definitely. That was amazing of her.

1412
00:52:10,582 --> 00:52:14,352
(He helped his mother clear up her desk.)

1413
00:52:14,892 --> 00:52:15,951
(He stayed with her during her last moment at work.)

1414
00:52:15,952 --> 00:52:17,651
She looked so nice.

1415
00:52:17,652 --> 00:52:19,891
In September, there's my birthday,

1416
00:52:19,892 --> 00:52:21,261
my grandmother's death anniversary,

1417
00:52:21,262 --> 00:52:24,292
and Chuseok happens around that time as well.

1418
00:52:24,332 --> 00:52:25,832
I wanted to take care of all of those dates,

1419
00:52:26,002 --> 00:52:28,331
but we were always busy during Chuseok.

1420
00:52:28,332 --> 00:52:32,202
I haven't been able to spend time for my birthday for a while.

1421
00:52:32,402 --> 00:52:34,241
While I was there, I wanted to spend time for my birthday...

1422
00:52:34,242 --> 00:52:35,371
and visit my grandmother...

1423
00:52:35,372 --> 00:52:37,242
and grandfather.

1424
00:52:37,272 --> 00:52:38,941
- That's great. - That's great...

1425
00:52:38,942 --> 00:52:40,011
to spend time on your birthday with your mother.

1426
00:52:40,012 --> 00:52:41,481
The one who should be congratulated during that person's birthday...

1427
00:52:41,482 --> 00:52:42,511
is that person's mother.

1428
00:52:42,512 --> 00:52:44,282
Right.

1429
00:52:45,052 --> 00:52:47,521
- Should we use one umbrella? - We could use mine.

1430
00:52:47,522 --> 00:52:48,791
- Do you want to put that away? - Okay.

1431
00:52:48,792 --> 00:52:50,122
Put in here.

1432
00:52:50,152 --> 00:52:51,322
Give me just a moment.

1433
00:52:52,162 --> 00:52:53,821
- Their shirts match! - Right.

1434
00:52:53,822 --> 00:52:55,691
Gosh, did you two wear matching shirts on purpose?

1435
00:52:55,692 --> 00:52:56,831
No. It was a coincidence.

1436
00:52:56,832 --> 00:52:58,331
- Really? - No way!

1437
00:52:58,332 --> 00:53:00,002
- It was really a coincidence. - That's fascinating.

1438
00:53:00,662 --> 00:53:02,201
That happens all the time.

1439
00:53:02,202 --> 00:53:05,641
There are times when our outfits feel similar.

1440
00:53:05,642 --> 00:53:07,911
Do you share the same clothes with your mother?

1441
00:53:07,912 --> 00:53:10,111
No. That doesn't happen.

1442
00:53:10,112 --> 00:53:12,011
When my mom is wearing jeans,

1443
00:53:12,012 --> 00:53:13,811
I'm wearing jeans too.

1444
00:53:13,812 --> 00:53:15,311
That's great to see.

1445
00:53:15,312 --> 00:53:17,211
- Even the pants are the same. - Right.

1446
00:53:17,212 --> 00:53:18,752
- The outfits look really similar. - We really didn't plan for that.

1447
00:53:18,922 --> 00:53:21,022
Wearing matching shirts with your mother gives me goosebumps.

1448
00:53:23,092 --> 00:53:24,591
You should do that with your mother too.

1449
00:53:24,592 --> 00:53:26,461
- Wearing matching clothes with her. - We have different fashion tastes.

1450
00:53:26,462 --> 00:53:28,161
- What does she like? - Different fashion tastes?

1451
00:53:28,162 --> 00:53:29,292
What does she like?

1452
00:53:30,332 --> 00:53:31,431
Clothes for mothers.

1453
00:53:31,432 --> 00:53:33,861
- They'll look good on you too. - They'll look good on me?

1454
00:53:33,862 --> 00:53:35,801
- I should find something then. - You should.

1455
00:53:35,802 --> 00:53:37,232
All right.

1456
00:53:38,342 --> 00:53:39,802
- Should I put it in your bag? - Sure.

1457
00:53:40,272 --> 00:53:41,412
Open it up first.

1458
00:53:41,942 --> 00:53:43,242
- Okay. - There we go.

1459
00:53:43,642 --> 00:53:45,042
Hold on.

1460
00:53:45,342 --> 00:53:47,142
- Let's go. - Gosh.

1461
00:53:47,242 --> 00:53:48,952
They're arms are linked too.

1462
00:53:49,152 --> 00:53:50,911
You should do those things for your project of being a good son.

1463
00:53:50,912 --> 00:53:53,852
- Linking arms? My gosh. - Right.

1464
00:53:53,952 --> 00:53:55,792
That's really nice to see.

1465
00:53:56,092 --> 00:53:57,822
So the arms weren't linked to avoid the rain, right?

1466
00:53:58,022 --> 00:53:59,862
- What? - Gosh.

1467
00:54:00,662 --> 00:54:02,991
Don't pretend you understand him, Kian84.

1468
00:54:02,992 --> 00:54:04,392
Can you link arms with your mother?

1469
00:54:04,602 --> 00:54:07,102
It happens from time to time.

1470
00:54:07,262 --> 00:54:08,772
- Does your mom hold your arm? - She holds my arm.

1471
00:54:08,972 --> 00:54:10,301
- I pretend not to know about it. - But...

1472
00:54:10,302 --> 00:54:12,441
- You pretend not to know about it? - I just ignore it.

1473
00:54:12,442 --> 00:54:13,771
- Because it's awkward. - What is that?

1474
00:54:13,772 --> 00:54:14,842
Your body would stiffen.

1475
00:54:15,542 --> 00:54:17,842
When I met Grandma,

1476
00:54:17,982 --> 00:54:19,681
- it didn't rain at all. - Really?

1477
00:54:19,682 --> 00:54:21,181
I was worried about you being under the rain...

1478
00:54:21,182 --> 00:54:22,451
- while visiting the cemetery. - No.

1479
00:54:22,452 --> 00:54:24,082
- It stopped right when I visited. - Right.

1480
00:54:24,752 --> 00:54:26,451
It rained a little when I left.

1481
00:54:26,452 --> 00:54:27,991
- Really? That's a relief. - Right.

1482
00:54:27,992 --> 00:54:30,251
Not a single person was there.

1483
00:54:30,252 --> 00:54:31,262
Right.

1484
00:54:31,822 --> 00:54:34,461
As Chuseok is approaching,

1485
00:54:34,462 --> 00:54:36,461
- there will be a lot of visitors. - More visitors will come soon.

1486
00:54:36,462 --> 00:54:38,732
Right. It would be quiet on a day like today...

1487
00:54:38,762 --> 00:54:40,502
- without many visitors. - Gosh.

1488
00:54:41,332 --> 00:54:43,701
Have I grown, or has this place shrunk?

1489
00:54:43,702 --> 00:54:45,042
So this is how it's like?

1490
00:54:45,242 --> 00:54:47,002
This is downtown Daegu.

1491
00:54:47,202 --> 00:54:48,412
- Dongsung-ro? - Right.

1492
00:54:48,812 --> 00:54:50,412
Has it been around 15 years since I have come?

1493
00:54:52,042 --> 00:54:53,781
(Daegu's biggest main street and landmark in the 1990s)

1494
00:54:53,782 --> 00:54:55,651
Hotels, restaurants,

1495
00:54:55,652 --> 00:54:58,581
cultural activities, arcades, shopping, and department stores.

1496
00:54:58,582 --> 00:55:00,581
All of those things were enjoyed there.

1497
00:55:00,582 --> 00:55:01,822
When you live in the provinces,

1498
00:55:02,192 --> 00:55:03,452
there's just one place to go to...

1499
00:55:03,552 --> 00:55:05,591
- when you want to hang out. - Right.

1500
00:55:05,592 --> 00:55:06,891
- Downtown. - Right.

1501
00:55:06,892 --> 00:55:08,591
Everyone meets people downtown.

1502
00:55:08,592 --> 00:55:10,261
- There's nowhere else to go. - Right.

1503
00:55:10,262 --> 00:55:11,461
As of right now,

1504
00:55:11,462 --> 00:55:13,231
I heard that there were many other places to visit.

1505
00:55:13,232 --> 00:55:15,202
But it was only Dongsung-ro for me back then.

1506
00:55:15,402 --> 00:55:16,601
It's the place for the young generation.

1507
00:55:16,602 --> 00:55:17,631
I've heard about it so many times.

1508
00:55:17,632 --> 00:55:19,671
There's even a song that is called "Dongsung-ro."

1509
00:55:19,672 --> 00:55:21,441
During our times,

1510
00:55:21,442 --> 00:55:22,911
there was a group called the Dongsung-ro Sisters.

1511
00:55:22,912 --> 00:55:25,942
They became popular for singing on that street...

1512
00:55:26,282 --> 00:55:28,081
and that area became popular as well thanks to them.

1513
00:55:28,082 --> 00:55:30,151
Daegu has a lot of great food too.

1514
00:55:30,152 --> 00:55:31,522
- Of course. - Right.

1515
00:55:31,752 --> 00:55:33,952
(There are still many customers...)

1516
00:55:34,052 --> 00:55:38,762
(who look for the representative dishes of Dongsung-ro.)

1517
00:55:39,692 --> 00:55:40,821
There's the tteokbokki. Pepper tteokbokki.

1518
00:55:40,822 --> 00:55:41,832
Gosh, that looks good.

1519
00:55:41,962 --> 00:55:44,261
You know that many franchises started there, right?

1520
00:55:44,262 --> 00:55:46,031
- Mexicana and Hosigi. - Mexicana and Hosigi.

1521
00:55:46,032 --> 00:55:47,361
- Sinjeon Tteokbokki. - Sinjeon Tteokbokki.

1522
00:55:47,362 --> 00:55:48,832
- Seoga and Cook too. - Mexicana?

1523
00:55:48,972 --> 00:55:50,401
- Chicken franchises. - Tteokbokki as well.

1524
00:55:50,402 --> 00:55:51,841
- Right. - Really?

1525
00:55:51,842 --> 00:55:54,971
This place is the origin of Korea's fried chicken.

1526
00:55:54,972 --> 00:55:56,442
- Right. - Wasn't that Suwon?

1527
00:55:56,572 --> 00:55:58,342
- No. - Suwon is known for its galbi.

1528
00:55:58,542 --> 00:56:00,241
- No. - There's everything there.

1529
00:56:00,242 --> 00:56:02,282
- There are places to eat too. - Right.

1530
00:56:02,712 --> 00:56:04,052
Do you know your way around here?

1531
00:56:04,822 --> 00:56:06,321
- You don't remember this place? - I don't.

1532
00:56:06,322 --> 00:56:08,952
We met earlier at the square.

1533
00:56:09,322 --> 00:56:11,521
That was where Jungang Police Substation was.

1534
00:56:11,522 --> 00:56:13,322
- It must've been removed. - "Realize a just society."

1535
00:56:14,692 --> 00:56:16,762
- I see. - You couldn't tell at all, right?

1536
00:56:17,762 --> 00:56:19,661
- Things change whenever you visit. - Right.

1537
00:56:19,662 --> 00:56:21,132
- It's different all the time. - New places are built.

1538
00:56:21,972 --> 00:56:23,901
There's the sign for "Debec" over there.

1539
00:56:23,902 --> 00:56:25,172
You're right.

1540
00:56:25,402 --> 00:56:28,041
It's strange as it's closed now, right?

1541
00:56:28,042 --> 00:56:29,811
- Daegu's department store? - Debec.

1542
00:56:29,812 --> 00:56:31,242
(Daegu's department store is currently closed.)

1543
00:56:33,712 --> 00:56:35,311
(It was full of people in the past as Daegu's biggest shopping mall.)

1544
00:56:35,312 --> 00:56:37,582
- Gosh. - Look at all of those people.

1545
00:56:37,712 --> 00:56:39,651
It went wild for Christmas.

1546
00:56:39,652 --> 00:56:41,122
The atmosphere must've been great.

1547
00:56:41,452 --> 00:56:43,851
Another famous department store...

1548
00:56:43,852 --> 00:56:44,961
struggled because of this place.

1549
00:56:44,962 --> 00:56:46,661
- They all went there. - Right.

1550
00:56:46,662 --> 00:56:47,821
The people in Daegu only went there.

1551
00:56:47,822 --> 00:56:50,231
Strangely enough, the people in Daegu...

1552
00:56:50,232 --> 00:56:51,761
were really loyal to this place.

1553
00:56:51,762 --> 00:56:53,231
This place was a rendezvous.

1554
00:56:53,232 --> 00:56:54,501
It was a rendezvous...

1555
00:56:54,502 --> 00:56:56,471
and all luxury items were bought there.

1556
00:56:56,472 --> 00:56:59,241
Debec was in charge of many art competitions...

1557
00:56:59,242 --> 00:57:01,872
and dance competitions for the youth.

1558
00:57:02,272 --> 00:57:04,812
That building was huge back then.

1559
00:57:05,182 --> 00:57:07,012
- It was only that big. - Right.

1560
00:57:07,812 --> 00:57:09,711
That building looked small to me now.

1561
00:57:09,712 --> 00:57:10,712
Right.

1562
00:57:11,922 --> 00:57:12,952
It's KEY!

1563
00:57:13,382 --> 00:57:15,522
- It's KEY! - Hello.

1564
00:57:15,592 --> 00:57:16,991
- Hello. - Hello.

1565
00:57:16,992 --> 00:57:18,492
That's a great thing to see.

1566
00:57:18,522 --> 00:57:19,622
"KEY!"

1567
00:57:19,892 --> 00:57:21,662
His fashion looks extraordinary.

1568
00:57:24,062 --> 00:57:25,362
You're hungry, aren't you?

1569
00:57:25,632 --> 00:57:26,662
I am.

1570
00:57:27,032 --> 00:57:30,132
There's a famous gimbap place here. Do you want to go there?

1571
00:57:30,702 --> 00:57:32,442
- Really? Let's go. - It's right there.

1572
00:57:33,102 --> 00:57:34,771
- You've never been there. - I love places like that.

1573
00:57:34,772 --> 00:57:36,741
- It's her favorite place. - A restaurant loved by locals.

1574
00:57:36,742 --> 00:57:38,111
It has a clean taste.

1575
00:57:38,112 --> 00:57:39,582
It was a place I never went to before.

1576
00:57:41,412 --> 00:57:42,781
Hello.

1577
00:57:42,782 --> 00:57:43,851
Feel free to take a seat.

1578
00:57:43,852 --> 00:57:44,921
- Right. - Hello.

1579
00:57:44,922 --> 00:57:46,121
There's always a line here.

1580
00:57:46,122 --> 00:57:47,151
- Here? - Yes.

1581
00:57:47,152 --> 00:57:48,352
- Is that so? - Where should we sit?

1582
00:57:48,792 --> 00:57:49,792
Should we sit there?

1583
00:57:49,793 --> 00:57:51,391
Gosh, it looks good even by a glimpse of it.

1584
00:57:51,392 --> 00:57:52,892
Gosh, this place is good.

1585
00:57:53,122 --> 00:57:54,221
Do you know that place?

1586
00:57:54,222 --> 00:57:56,532
No. The rice looks good.

1587
00:57:56,762 --> 00:57:57,891
The rice looks good?

1588
00:57:57,892 --> 00:58:00,302
The burdock roots and the fishcakes.

1589
00:58:00,432 --> 00:58:02,602
We can tell right away by taking a look.

1590
00:58:02,872 --> 00:58:04,302
- What does that even mean? - What does that even mean?

1591
00:58:04,802 --> 00:58:06,741
- It doesn't make sense. - Well,

1592
00:58:06,742 --> 00:58:08,772
I just got too excited.

1593
00:58:09,842 --> 00:58:11,341
It was run for 47 years.

1594
00:58:11,342 --> 00:58:12,882
- Gosh. - The gimbap place?

1595
00:58:12,942 --> 00:58:14,311
- Gosh. - It was run for 47 years?

1596
00:58:14,312 --> 00:58:15,551
That's amazing.

1597
00:58:15,552 --> 00:58:17,311
We'll get a roll of gimbap and a bowl of udon.

1598
00:58:17,312 --> 00:58:18,581
A roll of gimbap and a bowl of udon?

1599
00:58:18,582 --> 00:58:20,782
- That's a great combination. - I've never heard about this place.

1600
00:58:20,922 --> 00:58:22,792
You wouldn't have heard about this place.

1601
00:58:23,622 --> 00:58:25,621
I came here often...

1602
00:58:25,622 --> 00:58:26,922
when I was working.

1603
00:58:27,622 --> 00:58:28,762
The food is great here.

1604
00:58:28,832 --> 00:58:30,131
It gets served right away.

1605
00:58:30,132 --> 00:58:31,291
The gimbaps were constantly rolled.

1606
00:58:31,292 --> 00:58:32,562
(Why has this place become her favorite place?)

1607
00:58:32,803 --> 00:58:34,072
It gets served right away.

1608
00:58:34,073 --> 00:58:35,273
The gimbaps were constantly rolled.

1609
00:58:35,773 --> 00:58:38,242
Gosh, that looks good. I liked old-style gimbaps.

1610
00:58:38,243 --> 00:58:39,843
- That must taste great. - That's perfect.

1611
00:58:40,113 --> 00:58:41,513
- That must be good. - Gosh.

1612
00:58:41,783 --> 00:58:42,882
That was amazing.

1613
00:58:42,883 --> 00:58:44,253
- I can imagine how the soup tastes. - Korean udon.

1614
00:58:44,713 --> 00:58:45,882
Korean-style udon.

1615
00:58:45,883 --> 00:58:47,852
(A tasty combination certified by his mother)

1616
00:58:47,853 --> 00:58:49,983
- Gosh, that looks good. - Thank you for the food.

1617
00:58:50,123 --> 00:58:52,152
Thank you for the food.

1618
00:58:52,153 --> 00:58:53,853
Give it a try.

1619
00:58:54,223 --> 00:58:56,163
I'll try the gimbap first.

1620
00:58:57,123 --> 00:58:58,863
It'll taste like the udon made in the past.

1621
00:59:00,063 --> 00:59:02,263
I'm not sure if you have tried this kind of udon.

1622
00:59:03,063 --> 00:59:04,373
(Satisfied)

1623
00:59:05,333 --> 00:59:06,603
Do you get what I'm saying?

1624
00:59:08,043 --> 00:59:09,302
- Well. - It's like the gimbap...

1625
00:59:09,303 --> 00:59:10,343
- for picnics. - Right.

1626
00:59:10,503 --> 00:59:12,543
- But it has a very clean taste. - Right.

1627
00:59:13,173 --> 00:59:14,542
Can't you feel the texture of the rice?

1628
00:59:14,543 --> 00:59:15,682
That must be so good!

1629
00:59:15,683 --> 00:59:17,782
- Right. - The rice is good.

1630
00:59:17,783 --> 00:59:19,152
That's the best gimbap.

1631
00:59:19,153 --> 00:59:21,152
It's strange how it's good without any meat.

1632
00:59:21,153 --> 00:59:22,352
(Enjoying a friendly conversation at his mom's favorite place)

1633
00:59:22,353 --> 00:59:25,022
It's not a shocking taste. There's no meat in it.

1634
00:59:25,023 --> 00:59:26,892
It just has fishcakes, burdock roots, and carrots.

1635
00:59:26,893 --> 00:59:28,062
- Gimbap for picnics? - Right.

1636
00:59:28,063 --> 00:59:29,222
- It's made in that style. - Each ingredient can be felt.

1637
00:59:29,223 --> 00:59:32,632
If my mom were to be a good cook in the 1970s,

1638
00:59:32,633 --> 00:59:34,803
it feels like the gimbap would've tasted like that.

1639
00:59:35,903 --> 00:59:38,302
You're so expressive. I can feel the taste right now.

1640
00:59:38,303 --> 00:59:40,472
Each grain of rice can be felt.

1641
00:59:40,473 --> 00:59:42,642
- Right. - Nothing tasted strong.

1642
00:59:42,643 --> 00:59:44,712
Right. Classic gimbap is great.

1643
00:59:44,713 --> 00:59:46,813
- Classic ones last forever. - Right.

1644
00:59:46,843 --> 00:59:48,942
(Tasting the gimbap of the past)

1645
00:59:48,943 --> 00:59:50,513
It must taste so good with the soup.

1646
00:59:50,783 --> 00:59:52,683
(He takes a sip of the udon soup.)

1647
00:59:54,883 --> 00:59:56,053
It's good.

1648
00:59:56,883 --> 00:59:58,623
- It's good, isn't it? - It tastes like old-style udon.

1649
00:59:58,853 --> 00:59:59,953
- Right? - Yes.

1650
01:00:01,463 --> 01:00:03,093
Should I get on the KTX or what?

1651
01:00:04,963 --> 01:00:08,433
(The taste that makes you want to head to Daegu impulsively)

1652
01:00:09,063 --> 01:00:10,133
It's good.

1653
01:00:11,533 --> 01:00:13,273
(It's good.)

1654
01:00:14,503 --> 01:00:16,403
Do you still have a lot of things to do after retirement?

1655
01:00:16,573 --> 01:00:18,472
- How has she been doing? - You seemed to be busy.

1656
01:00:18,473 --> 01:00:19,473
I'm busy.

1657
01:00:19,473 --> 01:00:20,473
- Really? - You're way too busy.

1658
01:00:20,474 --> 01:00:23,513
I kept being busy starting from last week.

1659
01:00:23,743 --> 01:00:24,913
Do you not feel bored?

1660
01:00:25,013 --> 01:00:26,983
I have to spend time with your dad...

1661
01:00:27,253 --> 01:00:29,522
two days a week.

1662
01:00:29,523 --> 01:00:30,523
(She's so romantic.)

1663
01:00:30,524 --> 01:00:31,823
They must get along really well.

1664
01:00:32,023 --> 01:00:34,893
I go to the cathedral to study as well.

1665
01:00:35,423 --> 01:00:37,463
I've been on trips almost every month.

1666
01:00:37,633 --> 01:00:39,263
- That must be great. - I'm loving it.

1667
01:00:39,893 --> 01:00:41,803
Don't I look much brighter?

1668
01:00:42,603 --> 01:00:44,532
- She looks relaxed. - I mentioned that.

1669
01:00:44,533 --> 01:00:46,632
Her complexion has gotten better.

1670
01:00:46,633 --> 01:00:48,772
It feels like her hair...

1671
01:00:48,773 --> 01:00:50,773
- has gotten more volume too. - Right.

1672
01:00:50,873 --> 01:00:52,572
I love that I feel so relaxed.

1673
01:00:52,573 --> 01:00:53,612
She must have had so many things she wanted to do.

1674
01:00:53,613 --> 01:00:54,613
Right.

1675
01:00:55,483 --> 01:00:57,613
It's great that you have come after a while.

1676
01:00:58,813 --> 01:01:02,153
I thought that it would be great for you to visit your grandparents.

1677
01:01:02,523 --> 01:01:05,023
Grandma and Grandpa would've loved to see you.

1678
01:01:05,323 --> 01:01:07,453
Right. I loved it too.

1679
01:01:07,593 --> 01:01:09,062
- Of course. - Right.

1680
01:01:09,063 --> 01:01:10,493
It had been years since I last visited.

1681
01:01:12,233 --> 01:01:14,233
- Are you done? - Shall we get going?

1682
01:01:15,163 --> 01:01:16,262
Let's go.

1683
01:01:16,263 --> 01:01:17,903
(Let's go outside and walk around.)

1684
01:01:18,433 --> 01:01:19,873
- I'll pay for it. - Okay.

1685
01:01:21,003 --> 01:01:22,902
- You two look like friends. - We're always like that.

1686
01:01:22,903 --> 01:01:24,373
- That's great to see. - Gosh.

1687
01:01:24,673 --> 01:01:26,342
My father...

1688
01:01:26,343 --> 01:01:28,282
wanted me to greet you instead.

1689
01:01:28,283 --> 01:01:29,812
(Feeling grateful)

1690
01:01:29,813 --> 01:01:31,512
- Since she's a regular. - Thank you.

1691
01:01:31,513 --> 01:01:33,413
- Goodbye. - I love those places.

1692
01:01:33,813 --> 01:01:35,423
Do you know the owner?

1693
01:01:36,223 --> 01:01:39,323
The owner here used to work in our hospital.

1694
01:01:39,793 --> 01:01:41,363
- Really? - Yes.

1695
01:01:42,463 --> 01:01:44,763
This much rain is okay.

1696
01:01:45,063 --> 01:01:46,532
- This will be a good memory. - Right.

1697
01:01:46,533 --> 01:01:49,203
President Jun, take a good look at those vibes.

1698
01:01:49,403 --> 01:01:50,602
The weather is terrible.

1699
01:01:50,603 --> 01:01:52,232
- No. - What are you saying?

1700
01:01:52,233 --> 01:01:53,572
- Matching outfits, - I see.

1701
01:01:53,573 --> 01:01:55,943
Linked arms, staying close to each other,

1702
01:01:56,243 --> 01:01:58,372
- and eating together. - It depends on the situation.

1703
01:01:58,373 --> 01:02:00,272
I'm sure Kian84 knows this as well.

1704
01:02:00,273 --> 01:02:02,543
Doing those things out of nowhere can have a negative effect.

1705
01:02:02,713 --> 01:02:03,842
Your mother might not like those things.

1706
01:02:03,843 --> 01:02:05,113
- Right. - We might get even more awkward.

1707
01:02:05,253 --> 01:02:07,052
It's better to look indifferent like Kian84 and I...

1708
01:02:07,053 --> 01:02:08,582
instead of forcing ourselves to do things.

1709
01:02:08,583 --> 01:02:09,752
- If we force ourselves... - But...

1710
01:02:09,753 --> 01:02:11,922
when I'm fine with my mom hold onto my arm.

1711
01:02:11,923 --> 01:02:13,292
- Are you drawing a line? - Are you drawing a line?

1712
01:02:13,293 --> 01:02:14,523
No.

1713
01:02:14,993 --> 01:02:16,523
We're just different.

1714
01:02:17,363 --> 01:02:20,132
- Right. You should draw a line. - What? We're similar.

1715
01:02:20,133 --> 01:02:21,632
I can't do things like carrying her on my back...

1716
01:02:21,633 --> 01:02:23,062
and saying things like, "I love you."

1717
01:02:23,063 --> 01:02:25,102
I can walk with my mom with our arms linked.

1718
01:02:25,103 --> 01:02:26,103
(Chuckling)

1719
01:02:26,673 --> 01:02:28,303
- Should we go this way? - Yes.

1720
01:02:29,403 --> 01:02:30,873
(Watching carefully)

1721
01:02:31,913 --> 01:02:33,473
What kind of place is this?

1722
01:02:35,343 --> 01:02:36,982
It's a place where you take pictures.

1723
01:02:36,983 --> 01:02:38,582
It's like the sticker photo booths in the past.

1724
01:02:38,583 --> 01:02:39,813
- Really? - Yes.

1725
01:02:41,023 --> 01:02:42,923
- The photobooth. - Do you want to give it a try?

1726
01:02:43,383 --> 01:02:45,153
Yes. Let's try taking photos too.

1727
01:02:46,453 --> 01:02:47,863
We can go in here.

1728
01:02:48,763 --> 01:02:49,763
Here?

1729
01:02:49,763 --> 01:02:50,763
(Full of excitement)

1730
01:02:50,764 --> 01:02:52,293
They have a lot of things here.

1731
01:02:52,393 --> 01:02:54,133
You're going to a photobooth with your mom?

1732
01:02:54,933 --> 01:02:56,563
- That's nice. - A photobooth with your mom?

1733
01:02:56,763 --> 01:02:57,973
I think it's great.

1734
01:02:59,273 --> 01:03:01,073
Oh, my gosh!

1735
01:03:02,303 --> 01:03:04,142
(Taking a look)

1736
01:03:04,143 --> 01:03:05,443
There are even combs prepared.

1737
01:03:06,143 --> 01:03:09,283
There are combs and hair irons.

1738
01:03:09,343 --> 01:03:10,883
- Really? - Yes.

1739
01:03:12,413 --> 01:03:14,482
- Have you been to these places? - I have.

1740
01:03:14,483 --> 01:03:16,823
- Really? - I've never done this before.

1741
01:03:17,323 --> 01:03:18,453
Take a look.

1742
01:03:19,093 --> 01:03:20,192
Let's take a look.

1743
01:03:20,193 --> 01:03:21,393
(Eyes shining)

1744
01:03:21,763 --> 01:03:23,062
Is there anything you want to put on?

1745
01:03:23,063 --> 01:03:24,793
Should I put on a hairband?

1746
01:03:25,133 --> 01:03:27,163
(He follows his mother.)

1747
01:03:27,293 --> 01:03:29,363
(I'll choose with you.)

1748
01:03:31,303 --> 01:03:33,372
It's a place she would never go to if it wasn't for her son.

1749
01:03:33,373 --> 01:03:34,373
- Right. - Right.

1750
01:03:34,743 --> 01:03:36,773
How is this one?

1751
01:03:36,903 --> 01:03:38,473
- Should we go with that one? - Yes.

1752
01:03:38,873 --> 01:03:40,473
- You should put this one on. - That's cute.

1753
01:03:40,773 --> 01:03:42,513
- Gosh. - Those ones are nice.

1754
01:03:43,413 --> 01:03:44,582
(Interested)

1755
01:03:44,583 --> 01:03:46,183
(What is it?)

1756
01:03:46,753 --> 01:03:47,953
(Grabbing)

1757
01:03:48,323 --> 01:03:49,853
- Should we put on the glasses too? - Yes.

1758
01:03:50,783 --> 01:03:53,253
We'll put the glasses on after the hairbands.

1759
01:03:53,623 --> 01:03:54,693
Right.

1760
01:03:56,323 --> 01:03:57,692
The children must lead them there.

1761
01:03:57,693 --> 01:03:59,293
Mothers would only go with their children.

1762
01:03:59,433 --> 01:04:01,062
(I can't watch this.)

1763
01:04:01,063 --> 01:04:02,602
There's a place for our belongings too.

1764
01:04:02,603 --> 01:04:04,003
- I'll put my purse down. - Right.

1765
01:04:04,633 --> 01:04:05,932
How is it done?

1766
01:04:05,933 --> 01:04:08,603
It's the same as just taking regular photos.

1767
01:04:09,043 --> 01:04:10,442
(Taking a look)

1768
01:04:10,443 --> 01:04:12,272
The bills have to be put in.

1769
01:04:12,273 --> 01:04:14,143
Credit cards can be used.

1770
01:04:14,613 --> 01:04:16,342
- Should we go with this one? - Sure.

1771
01:04:16,343 --> 01:04:18,113
(The son leads things smoothly.)

1772
01:04:18,413 --> 01:04:19,783
(She's just fascinated.)

1773
01:04:20,183 --> 01:04:21,523
- Where's the headband? - Here.

1774
01:04:22,653 --> 01:04:24,083
(Putting them on at the same time as if it was planned)

1775
01:04:24,993 --> 01:04:27,522
(He naturally makes a pose right next to his mom.)

1776
01:04:27,523 --> 01:04:28,692
(Smiling)

1777
01:04:28,693 --> 01:04:29,863
You have to look here.

1778
01:04:30,393 --> 01:04:32,393
Here!

1779
01:04:32,463 --> 01:04:34,333
You're really good at finding the camera lens.

1780
01:04:35,263 --> 01:04:36,662
(Embarrassed)

1781
01:04:36,663 --> 01:04:38,603
Try looking up here again.

1782
01:04:39,203 --> 01:04:40,273
There?

1783
01:04:40,633 --> 01:04:42,243
You're acting cute in front of your mom?

1784
01:04:42,903 --> 01:04:44,273
(His mother seems to look lost.)

1785
01:04:46,473 --> 01:04:48,182
- I keep looking at the screen. - Right.

1786
01:04:48,183 --> 01:04:50,443
Let's get a bit closer.

1787
01:04:50,683 --> 01:04:51,712
Here.

1788
01:04:51,713 --> 01:04:52,953
(Getting even closer)

1789
01:04:53,483 --> 01:04:54,823
(Reaching out her arm)

1790
01:04:55,253 --> 01:04:57,152
(She gently places her arm on her son's back.)

1791
01:04:57,153 --> 01:04:58,253
That's wonderful.

1792
01:05:00,393 --> 01:05:02,993
(Shy smiles of the mother and son who look just like each other)

1793
01:05:05,033 --> 01:05:06,532
- We have another one, right? - Yes. Take the hairband off.

1794
01:05:06,533 --> 01:05:07,533
Right.

1795
01:05:07,534 --> 01:05:09,603
- Right. One without anything. - Right.

1796
01:05:10,333 --> 01:05:13,633
(Holding each other a lot more affectionately)

1797
01:05:19,513 --> 01:05:21,512
- Their arms are over each other. - It feels so natural.

1798
01:05:21,513 --> 01:05:22,612
This is it!

1799
01:05:22,613 --> 01:05:23,742
Gosh.

1800
01:05:23,743 --> 01:05:25,482
- Why can't you do that? - Exactly.

1801
01:05:25,483 --> 01:05:27,553
- It's a fight against time. - Gosh, we're so busy.

1802
01:05:28,453 --> 01:05:29,553
Is it like this?

1803
01:05:30,323 --> 01:05:31,992
(Even though they feel busy with the counter, )

1804
01:05:31,993 --> 01:05:33,093
Done.

1805
01:05:34,123 --> 01:05:35,192
(they do their best to pose for each take.)

1806
01:05:35,193 --> 01:05:36,193
There's the heart.

1807
01:05:36,263 --> 01:05:37,723
My goodness.

1808
01:05:38,433 --> 01:05:39,662
- That's a tough one to do. - One more time?

1809
01:05:39,663 --> 01:05:41,303
Is it over?

1810
01:05:42,033 --> 01:05:43,133
Smile.

1811
